          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              We may suggestively summarize example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TraditionalGeneralFoliation"}
              in words as:
            %p
              “In cohesive higher geometry, every foliation is a
              = succeed ".\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+foliation"} simple foliation
            %p
              Because the quotient map to the leaf space of a general foliation is always a
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersion"} submersion
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+smooth+morphism"} formally smooth morphism
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              If the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
              in example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TraditionalGeneralFoliation"}
              is not an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+groupoid"} étale groupoid
              to start with but a more general
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi LeafDec
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LeafDec_{\mathcal{D}}(\mathcal{G}_0)
              in general retains information of non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropy+groups"} isotropy groups
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
            %p
              We might decide to rule out this possibility by adding to the axioms in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FoliationInDifferentialCohesion"}
              the clause that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X//\mathcal{G}
              (here
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0//\mathcal{D}
            %p
              However, we might also keep that case and regard it as the first instance of what is certainly a natural phenomenon as we pass to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            Finally, given the above it is clear how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+submanifold"} isotropic
            appear in the cohesive axiomatics.
          .num_example
